{{> head}}
{{> nav}}
<main id="main">

  <section class="section hero scheme-dark case-hero" aria-labelledby="ux-audit-title">
    <div class="container hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__copy">
        <div class="stack">
          <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>AI-assisted UX audit</p>
          <h1 class="h1" id="ux-audit-title" data-split>An AI-assisted UX audit, delivered as a <span class="mark">product<svg class="mark__svg" viewBox="0 0 240 24" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" data-draw><path d="M4 15 C 46 8, 92 19, 136 12 S 210 7, 236 13"/></svg></span></h1>
          <p class="text-lg" data-reveal>For startups with a live app or SaaS product. AI agents audit it end to end: UX, UI, design system, accessibility, motion, copy and handoff. A senior designer judges every finding. You get an interactive product, not a PDF, that tells your team which issue to fix first and when.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="actions actions--center" data-reveal>
          <a class="btn btn--lime" href="{{calendar}}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="btn__label"><span data-text="Book a Call">Book a Call</span></span></a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost-dark" href="/work/echopod-ux-audit/"><span class="btn__label"><span data-text="See an example">See an example</span></span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-light" aria-labelledby="what-title">
    <div class="container split-rows">
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="what-title" data-split>What is a UX audit?</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <p>A UX audit is a structured review of a digital product that finds where people get stuck, where the interface is inconsistent and where the design and the code have drifted apart. Each finding is tied to a specific screen or flow, rated by severity, and paired with a recommendation, so the team knows what to fix first and why.</p>
          <p>Ours is AI-assisted and delivered as a product. AI agents do the checking across the whole product, every screen, token, transition and contrast pair, which no person could do by hand in the same time. A senior designer decides what matters. It covers the live product and, when you have one, the Figma file behind it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="check-title" data-split>What do we check?</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>UX:</strong> user flows, navigation, information architecture, usability and hierarchy.</li>
            <li><strong>UI consistency:</strong> the same thing looking and behaving the same way everywhere.</li>
            <li><strong>Design system and tokens:</strong> raw values that should be tokens, components, missing states and naming hygiene in Figma.</li>
            <li><strong>Accessibility:</strong> contrast, touch targets, focus and reduced motion, against WCAG AA.</li>
            <li><strong>Copy:</strong> labels, errors, empty states and onboarding text.</li>
            <li><strong>Motion:</strong> durations and easing against your motion tokens, and transitions that help or get in the way.</li>
            <li><strong>Dev handoff:</strong> how cleanly specs land in Cursor and in your engineering team's hands.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-coral" aria-labelledby="how-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-title center narrow">
        <h2 class="h2" id="how-title" data-split>How does an AI-assisted UX audit work?</h2>
        <p class="text-lg" data-reveal>AI does the legwork. A designer makes every call. It is the same human-in-the-loop process we use <a class="text-link" href="/ai-design/">across all our design work</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="cycle" data-reveal-group>
        <li class="cycle__step" data-reveal>
          <span class="cycle__num">01</span>
          <h3 class="cycle__title">Map</h3>
          <p><strong>AI</strong> lists every surface and user flow in the product.</p>
          <p><strong>Designer</strong> ranks the flows by how much they matter, core value loop first.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="cycle__step" data-reveal>
          <span class="cycle__num">02</span>
          <h3 class="cycle__title">Check</h3>
          <p><strong>AI</strong> runs the token audit, the motion audit, accessibility checks and a copy review.</p>
          <p><strong>Designer</strong> walks the product end to end and reviews it by hand.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="cycle__step" data-reveal>
          <span class="cycle__num">03</span>
          <h3 class="cycle__title">Judge</h3>
          <p><strong>AI</strong> drafts each finding with its context and evidence.</p>
          <p><strong>Designer</strong> keeps, rewrites or drops every one, and sets its severity.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="cycle__step" data-reveal>
          <span class="cycle__num">04</span>
          <h3 class="cycle__title">Prioritise and hand over</h3>
          <p><strong>AI</strong> places findings on a value and effort matrix, writes them as tasks and builds the report from one source of truth, so nothing silently drops.</p>
          <p><strong>Designer</strong> orders the plan, records the walkthrough and talks your team through it.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-dark" aria-labelledby="product-title">
    <div class="container ai-def">
      <h2 class="h2" id="product-title" data-split>Why deliver an audit as a product?</h2>
      <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
        <p>Most audits end as a long PDF of problems, and the team is left to work out where to start. We deliver ours as a product: one interactive file the team opens, searches and filters, built from a single source so every finding is counted and none drops out.</p>
        <p>It answers the question that matters after an audit: what do we fix first, and when?</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Start here:</strong> the five highest-impact fixes, picked by a designer.</li>
          <li><strong>Now (0 to 30 days):</strong> quick wins and trust fixes.</li>
          <li><strong>Next (30 to 90 days):</strong> the priority sprint.</li>
          <li><strong>Later (90 days and on):</strong> the bigger bets.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-light" aria-labelledby="get-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-title narrow">
        <h2 class="h2" id="get-title" data-split>What you get</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="checklist ai-get" data-reveal>
        <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12.5l4.5 4.5L19 7.5"/></svg><span><strong>Prioritised findings.</strong> Each one has context, the problem, a recommendation and evidence, rated from critical to low.</span></li>
        <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12.5l4.5 4.5L19 7.5"/></svg><span><strong>An interactive report.</strong> One file with search, filters, a value and effort matrix, and a now, next, later plan. Not a PDF.</span></li>
        <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12.5l4.5 4.5L19 7.5"/></svg><span><strong>A video walkthrough</strong> your team can watch when it suits them.</span></li>
        <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12.5l4.5 4.5L19 7.5"/></svg><span><strong>Fix-ready tasks</strong>, written so a designer or developer can pick one up without a meeting.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-royal" aria-labelledby="example-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-title narrow">
        <p class="tagline" data-reveal>Example</p>
        <h2 class="h2" id="example-title" data-split>The Echopod UX audit</h2>
        <p class="text-lg" data-reveal>Echopod is fictional: a real client audit, anonymised end to end, with the same structure and process and invented names and numbers. It shows the full report, from scope and caveats to the findings matrix and the plan. Read the <a class="text-link" href="/work/echopod-ux-audit/">Echopod case study</a> or <a class="text-link" href="https://youtu.be/a6h4D_BZkbM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">watch the walkthrough on YouTube</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-dark" aria-labelledby="ux-faq-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-title narrow">
        <h2 class="h2" id="ux-faq-title" data-split>FAQs</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq__list" data-reveal>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion open>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>How long does a UX audit take?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>Usually two to three weeks, depending on how many surfaces and flows the product has. We agree the scope before we start.</p></div></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>What do you need from us?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>Access to the live product or a test build, your Figma file if you have one, and a short call about your goals. A conversation with one of your engineers helps us judge the handoff.</p></div></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>Can you fix what you find?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>Yes, as a follow-on project. The audit ends with a plan your team can run on its own, or we can take on the fixes in Figma and in code through our <a class="text-link" href="/design-systems/">design systems</a> and <a class="text-link" href="/design-engineering/">design engineering</a> work.</p></div></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>Do you audit the Figma file or the live product?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>Both. The live product shows what users actually meet. The Figma file shows whether the system behind it holds up. Where they disagree, that gap becomes a finding too.</p></div></div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {{> work-next-audit}}
</main>
{{> footer}}
